  <regions type="content">
  <region left="18" top="9" width="441" height="24" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="true">Cooling Water Scaling &amp; Saturation Diagnostics in Wet Utilities</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="45" width="70" height="24" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="false">2026-10-20</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="81" width="500" height="136" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="true">PHREEQC Geochemical Engine: Industrial Cooling Water Scaling &amp; Saturation Diagnostics</p>
    <p bold="true">Wet Utilities Evaporative Concentration, Mineral Solubility &amp; Water Stability Modeling</p>
    <p bold="true">Authoritative SMath Studio Simulation (PhreeqcWrapper.dll with phreeqc.dat)</p>
    <p bold="true">=========================================================================</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="229" width="500" height="360" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="false">Industrial Process Context &amp; Thermodynamics:</p>
    <p bold="false">In chemical manufacturing, power generation, and petroleum refining, wet utility cooling towers reject massive heat duties by evaporating recirculating water. As pure water vapor escapes, non-volatile dissolved mineral solids accumulate in the recirculating circuit by Cycles of Concentration (CoC = C_circ / C_makeup).</p>
    <p bold="false"></p>
    <p bold="false">Mineral Scaling Hazards &amp; Retrograde Solubility:</p>
    <p bold="false">Calcium and carbonate ions rapidly approach thermodynamic supersaturation. Critically, Calcium Carbonate (CaCO3, Calcite) exhibits retrograde (inverse) solubility — its solubility product Ksp decreases as temperature rises. Consequently, hot heat-exchanger tube surfaces (35°C to 45°C) experience severe crystallization fouling, causing heat transfer degradation, flow restriction, under-deposit corrosion, and unscheduled shutdowns.</p>
    <p bold="false"></p>
    <p bold="false">Water Quality Indices Used by Plant Chemists:</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Langelier Saturation Index (LSI): LSI = pH - pHs ≈ SI(Calcite). LSI &gt; 0 indicates scaling tendency.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Ryznar Stability Index (RSI): RSI = 2·pHs - pH. RSI &lt; 6.0 indicates heavy scale formation; 6.0 to 7.0 is stable water; RSI &gt; 7.0 indicates corrosive water attacking carbon steel piping.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Gypsum Saturation Index: SI(Gypsum) monitors sulfate precipitation risk at high CoC or with sulfuric acid dosing.</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="601" width="500" height="88" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="true">=========================================================================</p>
    <p bold="true">PART I: MAKEUP WATER ANALYSIS &amp; BASELINE GEOCHEMICAL SPECIATION</p>
    <p bold="true">=========================================================================</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="701" width="500" height="152" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="false">Raw Makeup Water Quality (Municipal / Clarified Surface River Water at 20°C, 1 atm):</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Solution pH:           7.80 [-]</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Calcium (Ca+2):        80.0 mg/L (2.00 mmol/L)</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Magnesium (Mg+2):      20.0 mg/L (0.82 mmol/L)</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Sodium (Na+):          50.0 mg/L (2.17 mmol/L)</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Chloride (Cl-):        60.0 mg/L (1.69 mmol/L)</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Sulfate (as S(6)):     40.0 mg/L as SO4 (0.42 mmol/L)</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Alkalinity (as C(4)): 120.0 mg/L as HCO3 (1.97 mmol/L)</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="865" width="44" height="24" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <math>
    <description active="true" position="Top" lang="eng">
      <content>
        <p>Makeup water PHREEQC definition script (20 °C, 1 atm, raw concentrations in mg/L)</p>
      </content>
    </description>
    <input>
    <e type="operand">sol_makeup</e>
    <e type="operand" style="string">SOLUTION 1 Makeup Water; units mg/L; pH 7.8; temp 20; Ca 80; Mg 20; Na 50; Cl 60; S(6) 40; C(4) 120; END</e>
    <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
    </input>
  </math>
  </region>
