  <region left="18" top="7051" width="500" height="88" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="true">=========================================================================</p>
    <p bold="true">PART VI: INDUSTRIAL WATER TREATMENT SYNTHESIS &amp; MITIGATION ACTIONS</p>
    <p bold="true">=========================================================================</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  <region left="18" top="7151" width="500" height="568" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
  <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
    <content>
    <p bold="false">1. Immediate CaCO3 Scaling Hazard (The CoC Dilemma):</p>
    <p bold="false">  • In raw makeup water (CoC = 1), Calcite is already supersaturated at 20°C (SI = +0.222).</p>
    <p bold="false">  • At modest evaporative concentration (CoC = 3 to 4), Calcite SI reaches +1.17 at 25°C and +1.32 at 45°C. At heat exchanger skin temperatures (40°C), the Ryznar index drops to RSI = 5.60, entering the heavy scaling zone.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Uninhibited operation above CoC = 2 will cause rapid shell-and-tube exchanger fouling, leading to severe head loss, lost condensing vacuum, and compressor trip.</p>
    <p bold="false"></p>
    <p bold="false">2. Scale Inhibitor Dosing Strategy (Phosphonate / Polyacrylate Chemistry):</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Modern cooling water treatment programs use threshold scale inhibitors (such as PBTC, HEDP, and polyacrylic polymers).</p>
    <p bold="false">  • These antiscalants distort CaCO3 crystal lattice growth and allow the cooling loop to operate stably at supersaturated conditions up to LSI ≈ +2.0 to +2.5 without precipitation.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • For this makeup water, an antiscalant program enables operating at CoC = 4 to 6 with zero scale deposition.</p>
    <p bold="false"></p>
    <p bold="false">3. Acid Treatment for High CoC Operation (H2SO4 Dosing):</p>
    <p bold="false">  • If higher concentration cycles (CoC &gt; 6) are targeted to conserve fresh water, sulfuric acid (H2SO4) dosing is introduced into the cooling tower basin.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Acid neutralizes bicarbonate alkalinity (HCO3- + H+ -&gt; CO2 + H2O), depressing pH and holding LSI between +0.2 and +0.5.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Caution: Acid addition increases sulfate (SO4-2) concentration; SI(Gypsum) must be monitored to ensure it remains below -0.20 to avoid catastrophic calcium sulfate scaling (which cannot be removed by acid washing).</p>
    <p bold="false"></p>
    <p bold="false">4. Optimal Blowdown Control &amp; Water Conservation:</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Operating at CoC = 4 reduces blowdown volume by 75% compared to once-through operation, saving substantial pumping energy.</p>
    <p bold="false">  • Pushing beyond CoC = 6 yields diminishing water conservation returns while exponentially increasing chemical treatment costs and scaling risk. CoC = 4.0 to 5.0 represents the robust techno-economic optimum.</p>
    </content>
  </text>
  </region>
  </regions>
</worksheet>
